{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The <special linear group over a finite field> $SL_n(q)$ consists of the determinant-one invertible matrices over $\mathbb F_q$. Counting an ordered <basis> gives
$$
|GL_n(q)|=\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}(q^n-q^i)
=q^{n(n-1)/2}\prod_{j=1}^n(q^j-1).
$$
The <determinant> map is onto $\mathbb F_q^*$, so
$$
\boxed{|SL_n(q)|=q^{n(n-1)/2}\prod_{j=2}^n(q^j-1).}
$$
A <transvection> has the form $I+vf$, where $v\ne0$, the <linear functional> $f$ is nonzero and $f(v)=0$. It has <determinant> one, fixes the <hyperplane> $\ker f$ pointwise, and its displacement has image the line $\langle v\rangle$. In particular $E_{ij}(t)=I+te_{ij}$ for $i\ne j$ is an elementary <transvection> when $t\ne0$.

Row addition is multiplication by such a <matrix>. <Gaussian elimination> reduces a determinant-one <matrix> to diagonal form using row additions and determinant-one pivot exchanges. The latter, and all remaining diagonal factors, are themselves products of <transvections>: within two coordinates,
$$
w(t)=E_{12}(t)E_{21}(-t^{-1})E_{12}(t)=\begin{pmatrix}0&t\\-t^{-1}&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
w(t)w(-1)=\operatorname{diag}(t,t^{-1}).
$$
A diagonal <matrix> with product of entries one is a product of these two-coordinate diagonal matrices. Thus **<transvections> generate $SL_n(q)$**. This generation statement actually holds for every $n\ge2$ and every <finite field>; the exclusions in the question are needed for the subsequent simplicity assertion.

Commuting with all elementary matrices forces the <group center> to consist of scalar matrices $\lambda I$ with $\lambda^n=1$, so its order is $d=\gcd(n,q-1)$. The projective quotient acts faithfully on the one-dimensional subspaces. Its action is two-transitive: send an ordered pair of independent representative vectors to any other pair and adjust the <determinant> by rescaling a representative without changing its line. Therefore it is primitive.

The <Iwasawa simplicity lemma> says that in a faithful primitive action, if a <point stabilizer> has an abelian <normal subgroup> whose conjugates generate the <group>, every nontrivial <normal subgroup> contains the derived <subgroup>. In particular a nontrivial <perfect group> satisfying these conditions is simple. No proof of the lemma is required here.

For the point $\ell=\langle v\rangle$, the <subgroup>
$$
U_\ell=\{I+vf:f(v)=0\}
$$
is abelian, is normal in the line stabilizer, and its conjugates contain all <transvections>. Its image in the projective quotient has the same properties. Perfectness follows explicitly from elementary <commutators>. If $n\ge3$, choose a third index $r$ to obtain
$$
[E_{ir}(a),E_{rj}(b)]=E_{ij}(ab).
$$
If $n=2,q>3$, choose $t$ with $t^2\ne1$ and set $h(t)=\operatorname{diag}(t,t^{-1})$. Then
$$
[h(t),E_{12}(c)]=E_{12}((t^2-1)c).
$$
Every upper <transvection> is a <commutator>, and <conjugation> gives the lower ones. The <group> and its projective quotient are perfect. <Iwasawa's simplicity lemma> now proves
$$
\boxed{PSL_n(q)\text{ is simple for }n\ge3\text{ or }n=2,\ q>3.}
$$

The order of $PSL_2(4)$ is $4(4^2-1)=60$. Its faithful projective-line action has degree five, so its image is an index-two <subgroup> of $S_5$, necessarily $A_5$. Thus $PSL_2(4)\cong A_5$.

For $PSL_2(5)$ the order is $5(5^2-1)/2=60$. We construct a faithful degree-five action. In $SL_2(5)$ take
$$
i=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
j=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\4&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
They satisfy $i^2=j^2=-I$ and $ij=-ji$. Their quaternion <subgroup> projects to a Sylow 2-subgroup $V\cong C_2^2$. Its projective <centralizer> is $V$: a lift of a centralizing element conjugates each of $i,j$ to itself or its negative. In the <matrix> <basis> $I,i,j,ij$, those four sign choices each leave a one-dimensional space, and <determinant> one leaves just its two quaternion representatives. The <matrix>
$$
r=\frac{-I+i+j+ij}{2}=\begin{pmatrix}3&4\\3&1\end{pmatrix}
$$
has order three and cyclically permutes the three nonidentity elements of $V$. The <normalizer> quotient embeds in $S_3$ and has order dividing both $6$ and $60/4=15$, so it has order exactly three. Hence $|N(V)|=12$ and there are five Sylow 2-subgroups. Their <conjugation> action is nontrivial and transitive, and simplicity makes it faithful. Its image again has order $60$ in $S_5$, proving
$$
\boxed{PSL_2(4)\cong PSL_2(5)\cong A_5.}
$$

Finally,
$$
|PSL_3(4)|=\frac{4^3(4^2-1)(4^3-1)}3=20160,qquad
|PSL_4(2)|=2^6(2^2-1)(2^3-1)(2^4-1)=20160.
$$
In <characteristic two> an involutory <matrix> has form $I+N$ with $N^2=0$. Its Jordan blocks have size at most two. A projective <involution> in $PSL_3(4)$ has a unique involutory lift to $SL_3(4)$: its square is central, and squaring is an automorphism of the scalar center of order three, allowing a unique scalar adjustment. The nonidentity Jordan form in <dimension> three is only $(2,1)$. This GL-class remains a single SL-class, since the <centralizer> contains $\operatorname{diag}(I_2,c)$ of every <determinant> $c\in\mathbb F_4^*$. Thus $PSL_3(4)$ has one <involution> class.

In $PSL_4(2)=SL_4(2)=GL_4(2)$ there are two forms, $(2,1,1)$ and $(2,2)$, so there are two <involution> classes. More concretely, the first <group>'s single class has $21\cdot5\cdot3=315$ elements, counting image line, <kernel> plane through it and nonzero map between the quotient and image. The rank-one class in <dimension> four over $\mathbb F_2$ has $15\cdot7=105$ elements; the rank-two class has $35\cdot|GL_2(2)|=210$. <Isomorphisms> preserve conjugacy classes, so
$$
\boxed{PSL_3(4)\not\cong PSL_4(2),\quad\text{despite their common order }20160.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Let $V$ have <dimension> $2m$ over $\mathbb F_q$, with a nondegenerate <alternating bilinear form> $B$. The symplectic <group> is
$$
Sp_{2m}(q)=\{g\in GL(V):B(gv,gw)=B(v,w)\}.
$$
It acts freely and transitively on ordered symplectic bases. Choose the first vector $e_1\ne0$ in $q^{2m}-1$ ways, then choose $f_1$ with $B(e_1,f_1)=1$ in $q^{2m-1}$ ways. Their span is nondegenerate, so choose the remaining <symplectic basis> in its <orthogonal complement>. This gives the recurrence
$$
|Sp_{2m}(q)|=(q^{2m}-1)q^{2m-1}|Sp_{2m-2}(q)|,
$$
and therefore
$$
\boxed{|Sp_{2m}(q)|=q^{m^2}\prod_{j=1}^m(q^{2j}-1),\qquad |Sp_4(2)|=2^4(2^2-1)(2^4-1)=720.}
$$

For the <permutation> module $\mathbb F_2^r$ with $r$ even, put $\mathbf t=(1,\ldots,1)$ and
$$
W=\left\{x:\sum_i x_i=0\right\}=\mathbf t^\perp.
$$
It is invariant under coordinate <permutations> and has codimension one. The <dot product> restricted to $W$ is alternating because $B(x,x)=\sum_i x_i^2=\sum_i x_i=0$. Since the ambient <dot product> is nondegenerate, $W^\perp=\langle\mathbf t\rangle$. Evenness of $r$ ensures $\mathbf t\in W$, so
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{rad}(B|_W)=\langle\mathbf t\rangle.}
$$
Here the word symplectic must be understood as an alternating form before quotienting: a form with a nonzero radical is degenerate. The nondegenerate <symplectic form> is induced on $\overline W=W/\langle\mathbf t\rangle$, of <dimension> $r-2$.

Now put $r=2m+2$. Coordinate <permutations> preserve the induced form and give a homomorphism $S_r\to Sp_{r-2}(2)$. To see it is faithful for $r\ge6$, suppose a <permutation> $\sigma$ acts trivially on $\overline W$. For every pair of distinct indices,
$$
e_{\sigma(i)}+e_{\sigma(j)}\equiv e_i+e_j\pmod{\langle\mathbf t\rangle}.
$$
The two weight-two vectors could differ by $\mathbf t$ only if the complement of a two-element set also had size two. For $r\ge6$ that is impossible. Thus $\sigma$ preserves every two-element set, and hence fixes every index: intersect two such sets with one common element. Therefore
$$
\boxed{S_{2m+2}\hookrightarrow Sp_{2m}(2)\quad(m\ge2).}
$$
For $m=2$, both <groups> have order $720$, and the injection is onto. This proves
$$
\boxed{S_6\cong Sp_4(2).}
$$
The lower bound matters: for $r=4$ the action on the quotient has the <Klein four-group> as <group homomorphism kernel>. The construction is the <deleted permutation module in characteristic two>.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

A transitive <permutation> <group> is primitive when it preserves no nontrivial partition into blocks. A <point stabilizer> is maximal precisely when the action is primitive. The orbits of any <normal subgroup> form a <block system>, so every nontrivial <normal subgroup> of a faithful primitive <group> is transitive. This turns the classification of primitive <groups> into a study of their <minimal normal subgroups> and their <group socle>.

The <socle of a finite group> is generated by its minimal nontrivial <normal subgroups>. A <minimal normal subgroup> is characteristically simple, hence a direct power of a <simple group>. Distinct <minimal normal subgroups> commute. If a transitive <normal subgroup> is abelian it is regular: its <point stabilizer>, being the same at all points, lies in the <group homomorphism kernel>. In that case it is elementary abelian, and the ambient <group> is affine. If two <minimal normal subgroups> occur, each centralizes the other; the <centralizer> of a transitive <permutation> <group> is semiregular, so both are regular and the two regular <groups> are mutual left/right partners. These observations are central to the <O'Nan–Scott theorem>.

The theorem organizes finite primitive <groups> into the following socle/action types. In the affine type HA, the <group socle> is $C_p^d$, degree $p^d$, and $G=C_p^d\rtimes H$ with $H\le GL_d(p)$ irreducible. In almost-simple type AS the <group socle> is one nonabelian <simple group> $T$ and $T\le G\le\operatorname{Aut}(T)$; a <maximal subgroup> not containing $T$ specifies the primitive coset action.

In simple-diagonal type SD the <group socle> is $T^r$, $r\ge2$, and its intersection with a <point stabilizer> is a full diagonal copy of $T$. The degree is $|T|^{r-1}$, and the action includes the appropriate <permutation> of the simple factors. Compound-diagonal type CD replaces this one diagonal by a product of diagonals in disjoint collections of factors; it is a product version of diagonal type. In product-action type PA one embeds in a <wreath product> with a primitive nonregular almost-simple base, acting on a Cartesian power $\Delta^r$, so the degree is $|\Delta|^r$ and the <group socle> is a corresponding power of $T$.

Types HS and HC have two regular <minimal normal subgroups>, each isomorphic respectively to $T$ or to $T^r$ with $r>1$. Their socle consists of the left and right regular <groups>; the degrees are $|T|$ and $|T|^r$. Finally TW, twisted-wreath type, has a unique regular nonabelian <minimal normal subgroup> $T^r$ and a <point stabilizer> acting through a twisted <permutation> of its factors. This has degree $|T|^r$; primitivity imposes restrictive conditions, in particular $r\ge6$. These eight modern labels refine the older affine/almost-simple/diagonal/product description. The theorem reduces the problem to <simple groups>, their <maximal subgroups>, irreducible linear actions and the specified product constructions; it does not make every primitive <group> maximal in a <symmetric group>.

For <maximal subgroups> of $S_n$ there are first two elementary families. An intransitive <maximal subgroup> is the stabilizer $S_a\times S_{n-a}$ of an unordered distinguished subset, with $1\le a<n/2$; replacing the subset by its complement gives the same class. When $a=n/2$, this <subgroup> sits properly inside the stabilizer of the two equal blocks. A transitive imprimitive <maximal subgroup> is a full block-system stabilizer $S_a\wr S_b$, with $ab=n$, $a,b>1$, in its imprimitive action.

The <alternating group> $A_n$ is itself maximal. A remaining primitive maximum must contain odd <permutations>. The primitive part of O'Nan–Scott places it among full affine, diagonal, product-action, or almost-simple overgroups. Full affine <groups> have degree $p^d$; full diagonal <groups> have degree $|T|^{r-1}$ and socle $T^r$; full product-action wreath <groups> have degree $d^r$. Almost-simple cases require the actual simple-group <permutation> degree and its full <permutation> <normalizer>. Regular nonabelian types are contained in the corresponding holomorph/diagonal or product overgroups, so do not supply extra maximal families. Parity and exceptional containments must be checked before calling any of these candidates maximal.

In degree sixteen the complete list, up to $S_{16}$-conjugacy, is
$$
\boxed{A_{16};\quad S_a\times S_{16-a}\ (1\le a\le7);\quad S_2\wr S_8,\ S_4\wr S_4,\ S_8\wr S_2.}
$$
The three <wreath products> here act on blocks, not Cartesian coordinates. The other proper primitive <groups> of this degree are affine and lie in $AGL_4(2)$. A nonzero translation of its sixteen points is eight <transpositions>, and a generating linear <transvection> is four <transpositions>, so the full affine <group> lies in $A_{16}$. Even the tempting product-action $S_4\wr S_2$ on $4^2$ points is contained in this affine <group>: identify each four-point coordinate with $\mathbb F_2^2$. Alternatively, each base <transposition> repeats four times and coordinate exchange makes six <transpositions>, both even. Thus none is an additional maximum of $S_{16}$. There are **eleven conjugacy classes** in the displayed list.

In degree sixty the complete list is
$$
\boxed{\begin{gathered}
A_{60};\qquad S_a\times S_{60-a}\quad(1\le a\le29);\\
S_a\wr S_{60/a}\quad(a=2,3,4,5,6,10,12,15,20,30);\\
PGL_2(59)\text{ on }\mathbb P^1(\mathbb F_{59});\qquad D(A_5,2)\text{ on }A_5.
\end{gathered}}
$$
The notation $D(A_5,2)$ denotes the full diagonal <normalizer>, not just $A_5\times A_5$. It is generated on the set $A_5$ by $x\mapsto axb^{-1}$, an <outer automorphism> induced by odd <conjugation> in $S_5$, and $x\mapsto x^{-1}$. Its order is
$$
|D(A_5,2)|=60^2\cdot2\cdot2=14400.
$$
Its two alternating factors form the <group socle>. The <point stabilizer> intersects them in the diagonal $A_5$, of index sixty. Odd <conjugation> by a <transposition> of $S_5$ fixes its six commuting elements in $A_5$, hence makes $(60-6)/2=27$ <transpositions> and is odd. Inversion fixes the identity and the fifteen <involutions>, so makes twenty-two <transpositions> and is even. This verifies that the full diagonal <group> is not lost inside $A_{60}$.

The almost-simple maximum $PGL_2(59)$ has order $59(59^2-1)=205320$ and its usual two-transitive projective-line action. Multiplication by a primitive element of $\mathbb F_{59}^*$ makes one 58-cycle and fixes zero and infinity, so it too contains odd <permutations>. The simple <subgroup> $PSL_2(59)$ alone is not maximal. Degree sixty is neither a prime power nor a proper power, excluding affine and product/compound types; its diagonal possibility is $T=A_5,r=2$. The almost-simple primitive possibilities, besides the natural alternating/<symmetric groups>, have socle $PSL_2(59)$. Their full <normalizer> is the displayed $PGL_2(59)$. These degree-specific classification facts supply the exhaustive primitive part of the list. Altogether there are **forty-two conjugacy classes of <maximal subgroups> of $S_{60}$**: twenty-nine intransitive, ten imprimitive, the <alternating group>, and two further primitive classes.
